CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight
SCALE_REALISM: "Not yet established. The L0 artifact is small synthetic CPU-only stdlib code with N_PROBLEMS=60, K=20, 30 seeds, controlled q/r/phi, and clear z-scores; it supports the causal mechanism inside the toy generator. It does not yet show that real model outputs have the required correct-vs-incorrect fragmentation asymmetry, that real graders differ primarily in high-precision recall, or that an actual leaderboard flips under fixed samples. Reproducibility of the artifact looks adequate from the packet: pre-registration before run, fixed config, code/results files named, and corrected inversion disclosed."
RATIONALE: "I do not find a shipping OSS system among vLLM/SGLang/LMCache/FlashInfer/TensorRT-LLM that already implements the claimed mechanism. Those are inference/cache/kernel systems, not answer-equivalence voting systems; local clone searches for self-consistency/majority-vote/canonical/equivalence terms produced only unrelated canonical tensor/layout/config/path hits, except LMCache benchmark answer normalization. lm-evaluation-harness is relevant prior implementation surface because it ships exact_match and task-specific normalization, but it is an adjacent grader baseline, not the phi-conditioned variable-recall phenomenon. Wang et al. self-consistency (https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11171) and Ranked Voting based Self-Consistency (https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10772) are closest method prior art, but from the packet they treat answer aggregation as helpful voting/aggregation rather than a high-precision recall knob that can hurt accuracy. The packet's strongest evidence is the q=1.0 control: phi=-0.9 drops 0.982→0.666 with z=-28.2, so the negative slope is not an over-merge artifact; phi=+0.9 rises 0.097→0.681 with z=+53.5; ranking inversion is constructed with fixed samples, A-B changing -0.597 to +0.662. That is an eval-systems insight, not mere glue. I withhold green because all demonstrated workloads are synthetic and the ecological leaderboard hazard remains unproven on real canonicalizers/models."
